{"name":"Spooky","hex":"#d1d2bf","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spooky","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Eerie, or suggestive of ghosts or the supernatural.","Spooked; afraid; frightened."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spooky"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spooky (DJs)","blocks":[{"text":"Spooky are a British electronic music production and DJ duo consisting of Duncan Forbes and Charlie May."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Spooky_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Spooky_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spooky_(DJs)"},{"title":"Spooky (Classics IV song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Spooky\" is an instrumental song, originally performed by saxophonist Mike Sharpe (Shapiro) and written by Shapiro and Harry Middlebrooks Jr., which first charted in 1967, hitting No. 57 on the US pop chart and No. 55 on the Canadian chart. Its best-known version was created by songwriter James Cobb and producer Buddy Buie for the group Classics IV when they added lyrics about a \"spooky little girl\". The vocalist was Dennis Yost. The song is noted for its eerie whistling sound effect depicting the spooky woman. It has become a Halloween favorite. In 1968, the vocal version reached No. 3 on the U.S. Billboard Hot 100, No. 1 in Canada, and No. 46 in the UK."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/3e/ClassicsIv_LP.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spooky_(Classics_IV_song)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Spooky_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Spooky_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Spooky_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Spooky_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u